// The threaded schedule with persistent workers: fcpw4w16-threads.bonsai's
// parfor over the points handed to sixteen workers -- one per hardware
// thread of the performance cores compare.sh pins to, eight cores with two
// threads each -- that claim points from a counter until none are left
// (`persistent`, Aila and Laine's persistent threads), the workers `t`
// bound to the CPU threads (`w` is the program's loop over a point's
// walks). Against fcpw4w16-threads this measures the
// difference between TBB's partitioning of the points over the threads and
// a worker claiming one point at a time: the walks of a point vary in
// length, and a thread that drew a long point holds up only itself.
// Everything else is the single-thread schedule's, imported. compare.sh
// runs it as a threaded schedule: the driver's --threads for WoSX's side,
// pinned to the performance cores.
import apps/wosx/schedules/fcpw4w16;

schedule {
    solve_all.persistent(i, t, 16);
    solve_all.bind(t, CPUThread);
}
